\chapter{\texorpdfstring{Task Pipelines: \texttt{Task},
\texttt{TaskChain},
\texttt{TaskState}}{Task Pipelines: , , }}\label{ch26-task-pipelines-task-taskchain-taskstate}

The Task pipeline feature provides MuDSL's answer to a recurring need in
embedded DSLs: describing a sequence of staged computations that run in
order, can suspend themselves, and can resume later. Unlike the
value-level pipelines of Chapter 6, which thread a value through pure
transformations, task pipelines are concerned with units of work that
have names, side effects, and explicit state.

A task pipeline is built from three cooperating pieces.
\texttt{TaskState} holds the shared mutable context that every task
reads and writes. \texttt{Task} is a single unit of work, pairing a name
with a callable. \texttt{TaskChain} is an ordered sequence of
\texttt{Task} objects composed with the \texttt{|} operator. Three free
functions --- \texttt{run}, \texttt{suspend}, and \texttt{upon} ---
drive and instrument the chain.

The execution model is cooperative and single-threaded. There are no
preemptive threads, no worker pool, no scheduler ticking at fixed
intervals. A task runs until it either finishes or calls
\texttt{suspend}; the chain then either advances to the next task or
stops until the program calls \texttt{run} again. This ties directly to
the single-threaded design pillar introduced in Chapter 1.

\section{The Cooperative Execution
Model}\label{ch26-the-cooperative-execution-model}

Cooperative scheduling places the responsibility for fairness on the
task itself. The runtime never interrupts a task that is in the middle
of its work; the task must explicitly yield by setting the suspended
flag. This is the same discipline that underlies coroutines, generators,
and event loops in many language runtimes, but MuDSL expresses it with a
single boolean field rather than a full coroutine machinery.

The practical consequence is that a long-running task blocks the entire
chain. If a task spins in a tight loop or performs blocking I/O, no
other task will run until it returns. The programmer contracts to keep
tasks short, or to break long work into multiple tasks that cooperate
via \texttt{suspend}.

Because the model is single-threaded, tasks do not need locks on
\texttt{TaskState}. Every access to the shared map happens in the same
call stack, sequentially. This is a deliberate simplification: it keeps
the header dependency-free and makes task pipelines predictable to
reason about.

The chain does not run on its own. It only advances when \texttt{run} is
called. A program can build a chain, run it partway, inspect the state,
react to it, and then run it further. This is the suspension and
resumption pattern that distinguishes a task pipeline from a plain
function call.

Contrast this with the value pipes of Chapter 6. A value pipe is a pure
expression: input flows in one end, output emerges from the other, and
there is no observable intermediate state. A task chain, by contrast,
exists precisely so that intermediate state is observable, named, and
addressable by later tasks and by external hooks.

\section{TaskState: Shared Mutable
State}\label{ch26-taskstate-shared-mutable-state}

\texttt{TaskState} is the shared blackboard on which tasks communicate.
Its definition is intentionally minimal.

\begin{code}
struct TaskState
{
  std::unordered_map<std::string, Result<std::string, std::string>> results{};
  bool suspended{ false };
};
\end{code}

The \texttt{results} map is keyed by task name and stores the
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
that each task returns. This is the primary channel of communication: a
task writes its outcome under its own name, and later tasks read earlier
outcomes by name. The use of \texttt{Result} (Chapter 22) means that
both success values and error messages live in the same structure.

The \texttt{suspended} flag is the cooperative yield signal. It is
\texttt{false} at construction, set to \texttt{true} by
\texttt{suspend}, and read by \texttt{run} after each task to decide
whether to continue. Because it lives on the state rather than on the
chain, a suspended chain can be inspected, logged, or modified before it
is resumed.

The choice of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}unordered\_\allowbreak{}map}
reflects the addressing pattern. Tasks almost always look up earlier
results by name rather than iterating, so hash-based access is the right
default. The map is also the natural place for hooks to deposit side
outputs, as the \texttt{upon} helper demonstrates.

Note that \texttt{TaskState} carries no type parameter. Values exchanged
between tasks are strings. This keeps the pipeline composable at the
type level --- any task can read any other task's result without
templated fan-out --- at the cost of requiring tasks to marshal their
data to and from \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}. The
\texttt{Result} type carries the error channel, so a failed task's name
maps to an error message rather than a value.

\section{The Task Unit of Work}\label{ch26-the-task-unit-of-work}

A \texttt{Task} is a named callable. Its definition pairs a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} name with a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function} whose signature is
fixed by the state contract.

\begin{code}
struct Task
{
  std::string name{};
  std::function<Result<std::string, std::string> (TaskState &)> run_fn{};
  Result<std::string, std::string>
  run (TaskState &state) const
  {
    return run_fn (state);
  }
};
\end{code}

Every task callable takes a \texttt{TaskState\&} and returns a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The state argument is how the task reads upstream results and how it
writes its own. The return value is what \texttt{run} stores under the
task's name in \texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results}.

Tasks are constructed with aggregate initialization. The first field is
the name, the second is the callable. The name is significant: it is the
key under which the result is stored, and it is the handle that later
tasks and \texttt{upon} hooks use to address this task's output.

A task is not a coroutine. It is an ordinary callable that runs to
completion synchronously. Suspension is achieved not by pausing the
callable mid-execution, but by having the callable set
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}suspended = true\allowbreak{}} (directly or
via \texttt{suspend}) before returning. The chain then stops after this
task and control returns to the caller of \texttt{run}.

Because the callable is stored in a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function}, tasks own their
captured state by value. Lambdas that capture local context ---
configuration, buffers, callbacks --- are the normal way to build a
task. The captured state lives as long as the task, and hence as long as
the chain that contains it.

\section{TaskChain: Composing Tasks with
operator\textbar{}}\label{ch26-taskchain-composing-tasks-with-operator}

A \texttt{TaskChain} is the ordered container for tasks. It holds a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}Task\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and a \texttt{TaskPolicy}, and it is built by overloading the \texttt{|}
operator.

\begin{code}
struct TaskChain
{
  std::vector<Task> tasks{};
  TaskPolicy policy{ TaskPolicy::StopOnError };
};
\end{code}

Composition uses two overloads. The first joins two \texttt{Task}
objects into a chain; the second appends a \texttt{Task} to an existing
chain.

\begin{code}
inline TaskChain
operator| (Task lhs, Task rhs)
{
  return TaskChain{ { std::move (lhs), std::move (rhs) },
                    TaskPolicy::StopOnError };
}

inline TaskChain
operator| (TaskChain lhs, Task rhs)
{
  lhs.tasks.push_back (std::move (rhs));
  return lhs;
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{|} operator was chosen deliberately to echo the value
pipelines of Chapter 6. The visual symmetry is intentional: both
features compose stages left to right. The semantics differ --- value
pipes thread a value, task chains thread a stateful blackboard --- but
the surface syntax is uniform across the toolkit.

A chain is a value type. Passing it around, returning it, or storing it
copies the vector of tasks. Because tasks own their captured state,
copying a chain copies that state too. For most uses a chain is built
once at the call site of \texttt{run} and not copied afterward.

\section{TaskPolicy: Error
Propagation}\label{ch26-taskpolicy-error-propagation}

The \texttt{TaskPolicy} enum controls what happens when a task returns
an error.

\begin{code}
enum class TaskPolicy
{
  StopOnError,
  ContinueOnError
};
\end{code}

Under \texttt{StopOnError} --- the default --- \texttt{run} breaks out
of its loop as soon as a task returns an \texttt{err} result. Later
tasks do not execute. The error is recorded in
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results} under the failing task's name, and
the caller of \texttt{run} can inspect it.

Under \texttt{ContinueOnError}, \texttt{run} does not break on errors.
Every task executes regardless of prior failures. This is useful when
tasks are independent cleanup steps, or when the chain is gathering a
set of results whose individual success or failure is reported in
aggregate.

The policy is a property of the chain, not of the state. The same
\texttt{TaskState} can be driven by chains with different policies. The
policy is set when the chain is constructed; the \texttt{operator|}
overloads initialize it to \texttt{StopOnError}, and a program that
wants \texttt{ContinueOnError} must assign it explicitly after
construction.

\section{The run Helper: Advancing a
Chain}\label{ch26-the-run-helper-advancing-a-chain}

The \texttt{run} free function is the engine of the pipeline. Its
definition is short and worth quoting verbatim.

\begin{code}
template <typename... Args>
TaskState &
run (TaskState &state, const TaskChain &chain, Args &&...)
{
  for (const auto &task : chain.tasks)
    {
      auto result = task.run (state);
    state.results.insert_or_assign (task.name, result);
      if (result.is_err () && chain.policy == TaskPolicy::StopOnError)
        break;
      if (state.suspended)
        break;
    }
  return state;
}
\end{code}

Each task in the chain is invoked in order. Its result is stored under
its name with \texttt{insert\_\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}assign},
which means running the same chain twice will overwrite prior results.
Two termination conditions are checked after each task: an error
combined with \texttt{StopOnError} policy, and the \texttt{suspended}
flag.

The variadic \texttt{Args\&\&.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}}
tail is a deliberate extension point. It allows callers to pass
additional arguments through \texttt{run} without the header having to
anticipate them; the arguments are accepted and discarded by the
variadic pack. This keeps \texttt{run} forward-compatible with richer
scheduling hooks.

\texttt{run} returns a reference to the state it was given. This allows
the call to be embedded in a larger expression, for example passing the
post-run state straight to an inspection routine. The return type also
documents that \texttt{run} does not copy the state; it mutates it in
place.

A chain that has been suspended can be advanced further by calling
\texttt{run} again on the same state and chain. Because \texttt{run}
always iterates from the beginning of the chain, the program is
responsible for clearing \texttt{state.\allowbreak{}suspended} before
resuming, and for ensuring that already-completed tasks tolerate being
re-run, or for trimming the chain. A common idiom is to rebuild a
shorter chain containing only the remaining tasks before resuming.

\section{The suspend Helper: Yielding
Mid-Task}\label{ch26-the-suspend-helper-yielding-mid-task}

\texttt{suspend} is the cooperative yield primitive.

\begin{code}
inline void
suspend (TaskState &state)
{
  state.suspended = true;
}
\end{code}

A task calls \texttt{suspend(\allowbreak{}state)\allowbreak{}} when it
cannot complete its work in this turn --- for example, because it has
issued an asynchronous request and must wait for the response. The flag
is set, the task returns a \texttt{Result} (often an intermediate or
placeholder), and \texttt{run} breaks out of its loop after this task.

Suspension is a signal, not a continuation. There is no stored
resumption point; the task's callable has already returned by the time
\texttt{run} observes the flag. Resuming means calling \texttt{run}
again, which re-enters the chain from the top. The task that suspended
is expected to detect, on re-entry, that its earlier work is recorded in
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results} or in external state it captured,
and to skip or continue accordingly.

This design avoids the machinery of coroutines while preserving the
cooperative scheduling discipline. The cost is that the programmer must
encode resumption logic explicitly, typically by checking whether an
upstream or external result is already present before performing the
suspended work again.

Because the flag lives on the state, suspension is observable. A driver
loop can poll \texttt{state.\allowbreak{}suspended} to decide whether to
sleep, to perform other work, or to wake the chain. The flag is the only
contract between the task and the driver.

\section{The upon Helper: Event-Conditioned
Hooks}\label{ch26-the-upon-helper-event-conditioned-hooks}

\texttt{upon} registers a hook that fires when an event predicate
becomes true. Its definition is compact.

\begin{code}
template <typename EventFn>
void
upon (TaskState &state, EventFn &&event, Task task)
{
  if (event ())
    state.results[task.name] = task.run (state);
}
\end{code}

The hook is a pair: a predicate callable \texttt{event} and a
\texttt{Task}. When \texttt{upon} is called, the predicate is evaluated
immediately. If it returns \texttt{true}, the task is run and its result
is stored under the task's name, exactly as \texttt{run} would store it.

The predicate typically inspects \texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results} to
decide whether its condition holds. Because the state map is the
canonical record of what has happened, predicates are written as queries
against that map. The example shipped with the toolkit checks whether
the \texttt{inspect} task succeeded and produced the value
\texttt{"even"} before running the \texttt{normalize} task.

\texttt{upon} is a single-shot check, not a persistent subscription. It
evaluates its predicate once, at the moment it is called. To implement
continuous monitoring, a driver loop must call \texttt{upon} repeatedly,
or the program must call \texttt{upon} at the points where the event
could have occurred --- typically after each \texttt{run}.

The ordering rule follows from this: \texttt{upon} hooks must be
registered after the events they observe have had a chance to occur.
Registering a hook before \texttt{run} and expecting it to fire
retroactively is a mistake; the predicate is evaluated at registration
time, not deferred. A program that wants a hook to fire whenever a task
completes should call \texttt{upon} immediately after the relevant
\texttt{run}.

\section{Value and Context Flow Between
Tasks}\label{ch26-value-and-context-flow-between-tasks}

The pipeline does not thread a typed value from stage to stage. Instead,
every task reads from and writes to the shared \texttt{results} map.
This is the central consequence of the \texttt{TaskState} design.

A task accesses upstream output by looking up the upstream task's name
in \texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results}. The lookup yields a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which the task must check for \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ok} before
unwrapping. A common pattern is to return an \texttt{err} result
immediately if an upstream result is missing or failed, propagating the
failure forward under the chain's policy.

The output of a task is its return value, stored under its own name.
Tasks do not write into other tasks' names directly; they write their
own result and let later tasks read it. This naming discipline keeps the
data flow readable and prevents accidental aliasing.

Because values are strings, tasks that work with structured data must
marshal it. A task that produces an integer will convert it to a string
on output; a downstream task will parse it back. The cost is acceptable
for the orchestration layer that task pipelines occupy, and it keeps the
types uniform across heterogeneous stages.

The \texttt{Result} wrapper means that the error channel is always
available. A task that cannot proceed returns \texttt{err} with a
message; the chain's policy decides whether later tasks run. This
integrates with the error-flow discipline of Chapter 22 without
requiring the pipeline to be templated on the value type.

\section{A Three-Stage Pipeline: Load, Transform,
Store}\label{ch26-a-three-stage-pipeline-load-transform-store}

The canonical task pipeline is a load-transform-store sequence. Each
stage is a \texttt{Task}, and they are composed with \texttt{|}.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
  using Result = dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>;

  auto load = dsl::Task{
      "load",
      [](dsl::TaskState &state) {
        state.results["source"] = Result::from_ok("42");
        return Result::from_ok("loaded");
      }};

  auto transform = dsl::Task{
      "transform",
      [](dsl::TaskState &state) {
        auto it = state.results.find("source");
        if (it == state.results.end() || it->second.is_err())
          return Result::from_err("no source");
        int v = std::stoi(it->second.unwrap());
        return Result::from_ok(std::to_string(v * 2));
      }};

  auto store = dsl::Task{
      "store",
      [](dsl::TaskState &state) {
        auto it = state.results.find("transform");
        if (it == state.results.end() || it->second.is_err())
          return Result::from_err("nothing to store");
        return Result::from_ok("stored:" + it->second.unwrap());
      }};

  auto chain = load | transform | store;
  dsl::TaskState state{};
  dsl::run(state, chain);

  for (const auto &entry : state.results)
    std::cout << entry.first << " -> "
              << (entry.second.is_ok() ? entry.second.unwrap_or("?")
                                       : entry.second.unwrap_or("?"))
              << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{load} task seeds the state with a source value. The
\texttt{transform} task reads that source, doubles it, and returns the
result under its own name. The \texttt{store} task reads the transformed
value and returns a confirmation string. Each stage's name is the key
under which downstream stages find its output.

Note how each task defensively checks for the presence and success of
its upstream. This is the pipeline's equivalent of null-checking:
because the map is shared and mutable, a task cannot assume its
predecessor ran. The defensive check is what makes tasks composable in
arbitrary orders.

The final loop prints every recorded result. The map contains entries
for \texttt{source} (written by \texttt{load}), \texttt{load},
\texttt{transform}, and \texttt{store}. This illustrates a useful
property: tasks can deposit side results under arbitrary keys, not only
their own name, which lets a single task publish multiple outputs.

\section{Suspending on a Simulated Async
Boundary}\label{ch26-suspending-on-a-simulated-async-boundary}

Suppose the \texttt{load} stage must wait for an external event that is
not yet available. The task can \texttt{suspend}, returning control to
the driver, which later calls \texttt{run} again.

\begin{code}
using Result = dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>;

dsl::TaskState state{};
bool external_ready = false;

auto load = dsl::Task{
    "load",
    [&](dsl::TaskState &state) {
      if (!external_ready) {
        dsl::suspend(state);
        return Result::from_ok("pending");
      }
      state.results["source"] = Result::from_ok("42");
      return Result::from_ok("loaded");
    }};

auto echo = dsl::Task{
    "echo",
    [](dsl::TaskState &state) {
      auto it = state.results.find("source");
      if (it == state.results.end() || it->second.is_err())
        return Result::from_err("no source");
      return Result::from_ok(it->second.unwrap());
    }};

auto chain = load | echo;
dsl::run(state, chain);          // load suspends, echo does not run
assert(state.suspended);

external_ready = true;
state.suspended = false;
dsl::run(state, chain);          // load now completes, echo runs
\end{code}

On the first call to \texttt{run}, \texttt{load} finds the external flag
false, calls \texttt{suspend}, and returns a placeholder. \texttt{run}
observes the flag and breaks before \texttt{echo}. The driver then sets
the external flag, clears the suspended flag, and calls \texttt{run}
again. On the second pass \texttt{load} runs to completion,
\texttt{echo} runs, and the chain finishes.

This pattern demonstrates the discipline required of resumable chains.
Because \texttt{run} re-enters from the top, the \texttt{load} task must
be idempotent in its suspended state: re-running it before the external
flag is set must produce the same suspension, and re-running it after
must complete. The check against \texttt{external\_\allowbreak{}ready}
provides exactly this idempotence.

The second pass re-runs \texttt{load} from scratch. Any side effect that
\texttt{load} performed on its first pass --- for example, writing to
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results} --- must either be safe to repeat or
guarded. In this example the first pass writes nothing to the map, so
re-running is harmless.

\section{Registering upon Hooks for
Logging}\label{ch26-registering-upon-hooks-for-logging}

\texttt{upon} is well suited to conditional logging or conditional side
tasks. The shipped example uses it to run a \texttt{normalize} task only
when the \texttt{inspect} task produced a specific value.

\begin{code}
dsl::Task normalize{
    "normalize",
    [](dsl::TaskState &) {
      return Result::from_ok("normalized");
    }};

dsl::upon(
    state,
    [&state] {
      return state.results.count("inspect")
             && state.results.at("inspect").is_ok()
             ? state.results.at("inspect").unwrap() == "even"
             : false;
    },
    normalize);
\end{code}

The predicate checks that \texttt{inspect} succeeded and that its value
is \texttt{"even"}. Only then does \texttt{normalize} run. If the
predicate is false, \texttt{normalize} is not executed and no entry is
written under its name.

A logging hook can use the same mechanism with a task whose side effect
is output rather than computation.

\begin{code}
dsl::Task log_complete{
    "log",
    [](dsl::TaskState &state) {
      std::cout << "pipeline reached: ";
      for (const auto &e : state.results)
        std::cout << e.first << " ";
      std::cout << "\n";
      return Result::from_ok("logged");
    }};

dsl::upon(state, [&state] { return state.results.count("store") > 0; },
          log_complete);
\end{code}

Here the predicate fires once the \texttt{store} task has recorded a
result. The hook runs a task that prints the names of all completed
stages. The ordering rule is respected: \texttt{upon} is called after
\texttt{run}, so the predicate observes the post-run state.

Multiple \texttt{upon} calls can be chained to layer several conditional
side tasks. Each evaluates its predicate independently against the same
state. The order in which they are called determines the order in which
their tasks execute, and a task run by an earlier \texttt{upon} is
visible to the predicates of later ones because both write into the same
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results} map.

\section{A Build Pipeline of Dependent
Steps}\label{ch26-a-build-pipeline-of-dependent-steps}

Task pipelines model build systems well, because build steps are
explicitly ordered, explicitly named, and frequently conditional. A
small build pipeline might configure, compile, and archive.

\begin{code}
using Result = dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>;

auto configure = dsl::Task{
    "configure",
    [](dsl::TaskState &s) {
      s.results["config"] = Result::from_ok("release");
      return Result::from_ok("configured");
    }};

auto compile = dsl::Task{
    "compile",
    [](dsl::TaskState &s) {
      auto it = s.results.find("config");
      if (it == s.results.end() || it->second.is_err())
        return Result::from_err("configure failed");
      s.results["artifact"] = Result::from_ok("app.bin");
      return Result::from_ok("compiled");
    }};

auto archive = dsl::Task{
    "archive",
    [](dsl::TaskState &s) {
      auto it = s.results.find("artifact");
      if (it == s.results.end() || it->second.is_err())
        return Result::from_err("no artifact");
      return Result::from_ok("app.tar");
    }};

auto build = configure | compile | archive;
dsl::TaskState st{};
dsl::run(st, build);
\end{code}

Each step depends on a named output of its predecessor. \texttt{compile}
reads \texttt{config}; \texttt{archive} reads \texttt{artifact}. If
\texttt{configure} failed, the \texttt{StopOnError} policy would prevent
\texttt{compile} from running, and \texttt{compile}'s defensive check
would also catch the absence of \texttt{config} if the policy were
changed to \texttt{ContinueOnError}.

The defensive checks matter because they decouple the task from the
policy. A task written to check its inputs will behave correctly under
either policy, while a task that assumes its predecessor ran will only
behave correctly under \texttt{StopOnError}. Writing the checks is the
more robust style.

A conditional step --- for example, publishing only when the build is a
release --- fits naturally as an \texttt{upon} hook after the chain
runs. The predicate inspects \texttt{config}, and the publish task runs
only when the configuration is \texttt{release}. This keeps the main
chain linear and moves the conditional logic to the side.

\section{Error Propagation in
Practice}\label{ch26-error-propagation-in-practice}

The two policies produce different post-conditions. Under
\texttt{StopOnError}, the \texttt{results} map contains entries only up
to and including the first failing task. Under \texttt{ContinueOnError},
it contains an entry for every task in the chain.

\begin{code}
using Result = dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>;

auto a = dsl::Task{"a", [](dsl::TaskState &) {
                       return Result::from_ok("ok");
                     }};
auto b = dsl::Task{"b", [](dsl::TaskState &) {
                       return Result::from_err("boom");
                     }};
auto c = dsl::Task{"c", [](dsl::TaskState &) {
                       return Result::from_ok("ok");
                     }};

dsl::TaskState s1{};
auto stop_chain = a | b | c;
stop_chain.policy = dsl::TaskPolicy::StopOnError;
dsl::run(s1, stop_chain);
// s1.results contains "a" and "b"; "c" did not run.

dsl::TaskState s2{};
auto cont_chain = a | b | c;
cont_chain.policy = dsl::TaskPolicy::ContinueOnError;
dsl::run(s2, cont_chain);
// s2.results contains "a", "b", and "c".
\end{code}

Choosing between the policies is a question of whether later tasks can
usefully run after an earlier failure. A pipeline whose stages are
independent reports benefits from \texttt{ContinueOnError}; a pipeline
whose stages are strictly dependent benefits from \texttt{StopOnError},
the default.

A task that wants to suppress an error and let the chain continue can
return \texttt{ok} with a marker value instead of \texttt{err}, leaving
the policy to govern only genuine failures. This is the usual way to
record a non-fatal warning without halting the chain.

\section{Combining Pipelines with Other MuDSL
Features}\label{ch26-combining-pipelines-with-other-mudsl-features}

Task pipelines compose with the rest of the toolkit. A task's callable
can invoke a parser (Chapter 23), drive a rewrite (Chapter 13), or
evaluate a lazy expression (Chapter 19). The stringly-typed result map
is the integration boundary: richer values produced by other features
are marshalled to strings on the way in and parsed back out on the way
out.

For example, a task that runs a parser on its input might store the
parsed representation as a string for the next task to consume, or might
store only a summary and keep the structured value in captured state.
The latter pattern is common when the structured value is large or
expensive to re-parse.

The lazy values of Chapter 19 are a useful complement to suspension. A
task that suspends waiting for external input can store a
\texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} in its
captured state and force it on resumption. This separates the
description of the deferred work from the scheduling of when it runs,
which is exactly the concern of the task pipeline.

Contrast this with parser combinators (Chapter 23), which are themselves
a kind of staged computation. Parsers stage their work through recursive
descent, not through a cooperative scheduler; they do not suspend and
resume. Task pipelines are the feature to reach for when the staging is
temporal --- when work happens in turns separated by external events ---
rather than structural.

\section{Pitfalls and Common
Mistakes}\label{ch26-pitfalls-and-common-mistakes}

The first pitfall is a task that neither completes nor suspends. A task
that loops forever, or that blocks on I/O without calling
\texttt{suspend}, stalls the chain indefinitely. Because the model is
cooperative and single-threaded, no other task will preempt it. The
remedy is to break long work into tasks that suspend explicitly.

The second pitfall is registering \texttt{upon} hooks before
\texttt{run} and expecting them to fire. The predicate is evaluated at
the moment \texttt{upon} is called, not deferred. A hook meant to
observe a task's completion must be registered after that task has had a
chance to run --- typically immediately after the \texttt{run} call.

The third pitfall is re-running a suspended chain without clearing
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}suspended}. Because \texttt{run} checks the
flag after each task, leaving it set causes the chain to stop after the
first task on every subsequent call. The driver is responsible for
clearing the flag, and for ensuring that any task whose suspension set
the flag is prepared to be re-entered.

The fourth pitfall is assuming a task's predecessor ran. Under
\texttt{ContinueOnError}, or after a suspension, a task may find its
upstream absent or failed. Defensive checks against
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results} are the correct defense, and they
make tasks robust to reordering and policy changes.

The fifth pitfall is relying on a specific iteration order of
\texttt{state.\allowbreak{}results}. The map is an
\texttt{unordered\_\allowbreak{}map}, so iteration order is not the
insertion order. Tasks that need ordered output must sort the keys
themselves, or maintain a separate ordered list of names in captured
state.

The sixth pitfall is writing to another task's name. While the map
allows it, doing so destroys the naming discipline that makes pipelines
readable. Each task should write only its own result, plus any
explicitly side outputs under clearly side-channel keys. Downstream
tasks read by name; overwriting another task's name silently corrupts
that contract.

The seventh pitfall is ignoring the cost of string marshalling. Because
values are strings, a pipeline that shuffles large structured data
between tasks pays a marshalling cost at every boundary. For
orchestration this is rarely significant, but for tight inner loops the
value pipelines of Chapter 6 are the better tool.

The eighth pitfall is confusing task chains with value pipes. They share
the \texttt{|} operator but differ in semantics. Value pipes are pure
and stateless; task chains are stateful and side-effecting. Reaching for
a task chain when a value pipe suffices introduces unnecessary state,
and reaching for a value pipe when stateful coordination is needed
forces the state into external variables.

\section{Summary}\label{ch26-summary}

The Task pipeline feature gives MuDSL a cooperative, single-threaded
staging mechanism. \texttt{TaskState} is the shared blackboard --- a
name-to-result map plus a suspended flag. \texttt{Task} is a named
callable that reads from and writes to that blackboard.
\texttt{TaskChain} composes tasks with the \texttt{|} operator and
carries an error policy. \texttt{run} advances the chain one task at a
time, stopping on error or suspension. \texttt{suspend} yields control
back to the driver by setting a flag. \texttt{upon} registers a
single-shot conditional hook that runs a task when a predicate over the
state holds.

The model is deliberately simple: no threads, no locks, no scheduler.
Tasks are responsible for keeping themselves short and for encoding
their own resumption logic. The stringly-typed result map is the
integration boundary with the rest of the toolkit, and the
\texttt{Result} wrapper provides the error channel. Used within these
constraints, task pipelines are a readable way to orchestrate staged,
possibly suspending computations --- complementing the pure value pipes
of Chapter 6, the lazy values of Chapter 19, and the structural staging
of the parser combinators of Chapter 23.
